  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nUpdate...\nScattered showers and thunderstorms have developed along the\n925mb moisture gradient across SW PA. Increased POPs for the \nnext couple of hours as this activity moves SE across the area. \nThis activity should diminish toward midnight.\n\nA quasi stationary boundary was analyzed across NW OH to SW PA.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms continue, mainly across \nOhio, though the latest IR satellite imagery shows warming cloud\ntops and an overall decreasing trend. The latest meso analysis \nanalysis shows ML CAPE of 500 j/kg, and has been diminishing \nover the past couple of hours. 0-6km shear is also weak, at \n25kt. The severe potential has diminished, and the \nshowers/storms should also diminish through the rest of the \nevening hours. A few outflow boundaries were creating some \nmoderate cu, though the potential that these will spark \nadditional showers/storms was also decreasing with the loss of \ndaytime heating. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTonight, a ridge will begin to build into the region as the front \nremains stalled to the south. By Thursday evening, the boundary will \nbegin move northward across the region as a warm front. This will \nbring the chance for scattered showers and thunderstorms. Upper \nsupport and moisture is limited, though a few showers and isolated \nthunderstorms will be possible with weak ascent along the front. \n\nA quasi zonal flow is expected to set up across much of the CONUS by \nSaturday. A shortwave embedded in the flow, and a surface low/cold \nfront, is expected to approach and cross the Upper Ohio Valley \nregion Friday night into Saturday. More widespread showers are \nexpected as this system tracks across the area. There could also be \na few thunderstorms, though instability is expected to be limited \n(ML CAPE <500 J/kg) with minimal shear as well. Some uncertainty \nexists in how fast the surface low and cold front will move east of \nthe area on Saturday. If the faster scenarios were to happen, POPs \ncould be further reduced in the afternoon. Generally dry weather \nreturns Sunday and Monday before a stronger trough and surface cold \nfront approaches and crosses the region Monday night into Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures are expected to warm Thursday and Friday, with readings \ngenerally 10-15 degrees above average. More seasonable temperatures \nare then expected for the weekend with the passage of the cold\nfront.",
